## Japan — src/countries/jp/data/

Prefectures, and the eight regions built from them.

Licence: Open Data Commons Open Database License 1.0 (ODbL)
https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/
Source: (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, obtained via the geoBoundaries project
(https://www.geoboundaries.org/)

ODbL is share-alike for data, as with Poland: a modified version of these
boundaries must be distributed under ODbL, with OpenStreetMap credited.

The region boundaries are not a separate source. Japan's regions are not an
administrative level and no boundary set publishes them, so they are the 47
prefectures merged into the conventional eight groups - the same data under the
same licence.

## Russia - src/countries/ru/data/

Federal subjects.

Licence: Open Data Commons Open Database License 1.0 (ODbL)
https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/
Source: (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, obtained via the geoBoundaries project
(https://www.geoboundaries.org/)

ODbL is share-alike for data, as with Poland and Japan.

83 of the 89 subjects come from the Russia boundary set. The other six - Crimea,
Sevastopol, and the Donetsk, Luhansk, Kherson and Zaporozhye oblasts - come from
the Ukraine boundary set, relabelled, because the map follows the mapped
country's own official position on its borders, which is the same rule the India
map follows for Aksai Chin. Those six are recognised as Ukrainian by Ukraine and
by most other states. A Ukraine map added to this package would draw the same
ground as Ukrainian; the two country maps are not composable, which is a
consequence of the rule and not a mistake in either.

